OK, building a subagent-drain-with-drainMs-0 IT is feasible but non-trivial. Given all four threads are SUGGESTION level (verdict APPROVE, 0 blocking/important), I'll balance effort:

- Thread 1: implement per-bucket loop-steer in `_steerHeadlessLiveness` + IT in the liveness file (late wake → N notices). This is a genuine behavioral change, needs a test.
- Thread 2: implement shell-scoped kill switch via pure function extension (`shellDrainDisabled` param) + update pure UTs + an IT for the subagent leg. For the IT, I can build a small harness where a background `task` subagent is spawned and completes, with `shellJobDrainMs: 0`. Actually — I could add it to the drain test file... but it needs the task spawn machinery. Alternatively, check `agent_cli_task_completion_wake_test` AC-A: parent ends turn while children run; when they settle parent wakes. That already exercises the subagent drain with default config. A variant with drainMs: 0 asserting the drain still happens would prove the fix. Building: clone the AC-A harness with headless config drainMs 0. Let me look at the rest of that test to see the harness setup cost.

Actually wait — with drainMs: 0 and subagent active: my new action returns `drain` (legacy uncapped). The round: `Future.wait([jobManager.settled])` vs `_waitingSleep(wakeIn([], now, now))` = sleep(Duration.zero) — busy loop? Future.any with a zero-duration sleep: the sleep completes immediately on the event loop; Future.wait for a running child takes 120ms real → the any resolves via the zero sleep → round "completes" instantly without waiting! Then steer liveness (no shell jobs → nothing), `if (isBusy)` — not busy → skip. Loop continues → 10 rounds in microseconds → cap → `_finishHeadlessDrain` → still active (child running) → prints detach message + detach summary → run EXITS while child still running! That breaks the legacy subagent drain!

Hmm wait — is that also true pre-PR? Pre-PR the subagent drain loop was:
```
for (var round...) { if (!jobsActive) break; await Future.any([...]); ... }
```
Let me check the pre-PR shape... pre-gh-1459 loop presumably awaited `jobManager.settled` with the same Future.any pattern minus the ceiling sleep. The `_waitingSleep` was added by gh-1459 (the ceiling). With drainMs=0, deadline=now, wakeIn returns Duration.zero → sleep zero → Future.any returns via sleep. So the subagent drain with 0 would spin 10 rounds and detach. That makes option A (behavior fix) more invasive: when shellDrainDisabled and only subagents active, the round must await the settles WITHOUT the zero sleep — i.e. the legacy round = `await Future.wait([...settles...])` (no any/sleep).

So `_headlessDrainRound` needs to know whether the shell ceiling applies to this round. Simplest: keep semantics "the shell-leg kill switch": if drainMs == 0, the round skips the `_waitingSleep` alternative entirely:

```dart
final waits = <Future<void>>[
  if (subActive) _taskConfig.jobManager.settled,
  for (final job in shellActive) job.settled,
];
if (shellDrainDisabled) {
  // Legacy pre-gh-1459 round: subagent settles only, no ceiling sleep.
  await Future.wait(waits);
} else {
  await Future.any([Future.wait(waits), _waitingSleep(liveness.wakeIn(...))]);
}
