These are REPL-mode tests, not headless drain. For a headless subagent-drain IT with drainMs 0, I need runHeadless + a child that actually completes (not hangs). I can adapt the `_CompletionRouter` pattern: child does a delayed text turn (120ms) then settles; parent spawn turn ends; drain must await subagent settle + wake run, then exit 0.

Let me design the thread-2 IT:

```dart
test('shellJobDrainMs: 0 keeps the legacy subagent drain — an in-flight subagent still drains', ...)
```

Harness:
- Router: child run (assignment contains marker) → delayed 120ms text turn; wake run (payload contains '<task-result') → textTurn('results acknowledged'); parent runs → scripted.
- Lead: parent spawns background task via `task` tool. In the drain/liveness tests the lead used toolTurn with bash. For task spawning: `ToolCall(id, name: 'task', arguments: {'background': true, 'tasks': [{'name': 'fix503', 'task': 'child marker'}]})`. Check the task tool arg shape from waiting_children test: `taskTool(config: cli.taskConfig).execute({'context': 'ctx', 'background': true, 'tasks': [{'name': 'fix503', 'task': childTask}]}, null, null)`. For a ToolCall through the model, arguments presumably same map. In task_completion_wake_test, spawnTurns — let me check what the spawn turn looks like there.

Then lead's second turn: textTurn('delegated; waiting') — ends turn. Child runs 120ms, settles. Async-result notice steered into fresh turn (wake run). Drain: with drainMs 0 → action: subagent active → drain (with my fix) → round awaits jobManager.settled... 

Wait — timing: the drain starts after the lead turn ends. The child may settle before or after. If child settles before drain starts: `_headlessSubAgentsActive()` false → action exit → no drain; but the settle notice steers a wake run anyway (existing machinery), and `_finishHeadlessDrain`'s `if (isBusy) await _settled` awaits it. Either way the wake run executes before exit. Hmm — then the test would pass even WITHOUT the fix if the child settles before the drain's first action check... Actually without the fix (drainMs 0): first action: hasActiveJobs? If child already settled → exit → finish: isBusy (wake run pending) → await → wake runs → exit 0. So the test must ensure the child is STILL RUNNING when the drain checks. Make the child's work longer (e.g. 800ms) and the parent's spawn turn ends quickly. Pre-fix behavior: action → detach → finish: isBusy? The settle notice only lands when the child settles (at 800ms) — at finish time (~few ms after turn end) not busy → exit immediately WITHOUT the wake run. Test assertion: wake run context exists / last user text contains '<task-result' → fails pre-fix, passes post-fix. Also assert exit code arrives only after the child settle. Timing race: drain's first action check happens immediately after turn end; child still running (800ms window, registration+turn-end take tens of ms). Safe enough with a poll loop on contexts.

Actually even better: child work = gated — delayed until test observes? Simpler: fixed 800ms delay. The drain pre-fix exits in <100ms; child settles at 800ms → no wake run. Post-fix: drain waits on jobManager.settled (up to 800ms), wake run executes. Deterministic enough.

Router classification: 'noted.' fallback answers any stray run.

Contexts assertions post-fix: at least one context whose last user text contains '<task-result' (wake run). Pre-fix: none.

Also need `Unawaited` import? dart:async. The _CompletionRouter uses unawaited from dart:async.

Where to put this IT: test/cli/agent_cli_headless_shell_job_drain_test.dart? It's about the drain, sure — but it's a subagent case; the file is shell-job focused. Maybe add to drain file as it's the drain-contract file, title mentions. I'll add there.

Also update the existing 0-kill test title/comment? Its assertions stay valid.

Now thread 1 implementation detail. Current `_steerHeadlessLiveness` per job:

```dart
final action = headlessJobLivenessAction(elapsedMs: ..., quietMs: ..., lastConsumedBucket: consumed ?? 0, probedSinceLastConsumption: ...);
if (action == wait) continue;
liveness.consumedBucket[job.id] = elapsedMs ~/ quietMs;
liveness.seenProbeGen[job.id] = job.probeGeneration;
if (action == skip) continue;
... steer one notice with headlessLivenessElapsedText(elapsed)
```

New per-bucket logic:

```dart
final currentBucket = elapsedMs ~/ liveness.quietMs;
final lastBucket = liveness.consumedBucket[job.id] ?? 0;
if (currentBucket <= lastBucket) continue; // wait
final probed = job.probeGeneration != (liveness.seenProbeGen[job.id] ??= job.probeGeneration);
// A late wake (a long reaction run) crosses several thresholds at once:
// steer ONE notice per newly-crossed bucket (the ticket's "N crossings ⇒
// exactly N notices" budget), each stamped with its own threshold's
// elapsed. A model probe since the last consumption suppresses the whole
// burst — it was watching, not blind — but still consumes every crossed
// bucket, so no replay on the next wake.
liveness.consumedBucket[job.id] = currentBucket;
liveness.seenProbeGen[job.id] = job.probeGeneration;
if (probed) continue;
final tail = (await _shellJobs.tail(job.id, maxLines: 5)).trimRight();
for (var bucket = lastBucket + 1; bucket <= currentBucket; bucket++) {
  final noticeElapsed = Duration(milliseconds: bucket * liveness.quietMs);
  final message = '... ${headlessLivenessElapsedText(noticeElapsed)} ...';
  ...steer...
}
```

Wait — does the pure `headlessJobLivenessAction` still get used? Yes, for the single-decision path... but now the code computes buckets directly. The pure function remains used? I could keep using it for the probed/wait determination, but the per-bucket loop replaces its bucket logic. To avoid dead code, restructure: use the pure function per bucket? For bucket in (last+1..current): decision = headlessJobLivenessAction(elapsedMs: bucket*quietMs, quietMs, lastConsumedBucket: bucket-1, probedSinceLastConsumption: probed) → steer/skip. That's elegant: per-bucket decisions through the pure function. If probed → all skip. Consumed incrementally. Let me write:

