elapsedMs = 930000 = 15m30s. current=3, last=0 → loop buckets 1..3 → three notices. But the dump earlier showed only 2 runs. So the steer DID run with 3 buckets → 3 messages steered. But only 2 liveness RUNS observed?

The third notice: `_startRun` (bucket 1) then isBusy → `_agent.steer` ×2. Perhaps two of the steered notices got COALESCED into one run — e.g. the steer delivery batches multiple user messages into one context/run. The livenessRuns counter counts contexts; if notices 2 and 3 landed in the SAME context (one run carrying two steered user messages), the run's call-time last user text = the LAST steer ('15m') and there's only ONE context for two notices → livenessRuns=2 but 3 notices delivered!

Hmm, or the run for bucket 1 ('5m') ends so fast ('still waiting.') that by the time bucket-2's steer is processed the run already finished → bucket 2 starts a fresh run, bucket 3 steered into it... contexts: '5m' run, '10m'+'15m' run → livenessRuns counts both → 2 contexts but the second context's lastUserText = '15m' notice; the '10m' notice is the second-to-last user message in that context.

So "N notices ⇒ N runs" doesn't hold — steers into a busy run share one context. Is that acceptable for the ticket invariant "N crossings ⇒ exactly N notices (test counts)"? The notices ARE delivered (3 user messages). The count of notices in the transcript is 3; the count of RUNS is fewer. My test asserts run count == 3, which conflates runs with notices.

